# Copy to .env and fill in. Every value is optional; sensible defaults apply.

# ───────────── Provider & model ─────────────
# You do NOT set the provider/model here. Configure it once with Hermes' own
# wizard — it supports 40+ providers, AND a custom / OpenAI-compatible endpoint
# (e.g. a LiteLLM proxy or any OpenAI-style URL): pick the "custom" provider and
# enter its base URL + key. The teams reads that config directly.
#
#     hermes setup                                   # local / pip install
#     docker compose run --rm -e HERMES_HOME=/data/.hermes-shared teams hermes setup
#
# Optional, rarely needed overrides:
# TEAMS_DEFAULT_MODEL=         # force a default model name without the wizard
# TEAMS_FALLBACK_MODELS=       # comma-separated; shown in the model dropdown when
#                              # the backend exposes no /models list
# TEAMS_VISION_MODEL=          # separate multimodal model, only for a text-only
#                              # custom endpoint that can't read screenshots

# ───────────── Server ─────────────
# TEAMS_HOST=0.0.0.0          # set by Docker; keep 127.0.0.1 for local-only
# TEAMS_PORT=8000
# TEAMS_API_KEY=              # require this key on EVERY endpoint + the WebSocket — SET IT if you expose the port
# TEAMS_DATA_DIR=/data        # writable state dir (set by Docker to the volume)
# TEAMS_SWEEP_INTERVAL=10
# TEAMS_LOG_FILE=             # also write logs to this path (rotating 10MB × 5); stdout is always on

# ───────────── Browser handover ─────────────
# How a human completes an interactive login the agent is blocked on:
#   embedded (default) — drive the headless browser from a panel in the dashboard
#                        (works on a display-less VPS)
#   window             — pop a real Chrome window on the SERVER's desktop (only if
#                        the server has a display, e.g. your own machine)
# TEAMS_TAKEOVER_MODE=embedded

# ───────────── Browser viewer (how the dashboard SHOWS the team browser) ─────
#   auto (default) — use the neko WebRTC stream when Docker is available,
#                    otherwise fall back to the built-in CDP screencast canvas
#   neko           — require neko (errors → screencast fallback anyway)
#   local          — never use Docker; always the CDP screencast
# The neko mode runs one `ghcr.io/m1k1o/neko/chromium` container per team: real
# Chrome UI, 60fps WebRTC video + audio, and agents keep driving the same
# browser/profile over CDP. Each container needs ~1GB RAM.
# TEAMS_VIEWER=auto
# NEKO_IMAGE=ghcr.io/m1k1o/neko/chromium:latest
# NEKO_SCREEN=1920x1080@30
# IP clients should reach the server on for WebRTC (default: primary LAN IP;
# set to your public IP behind NAT, or 127.0.0.1 for local-only testing)
# NEKO_ADVERTISE_HOST=

# ───────────── Hermes ─────────────
# Only needed if Hermes is NOT pip-installed and you run from a source checkout:
# HERMES_AGENT_PATH=/path/to/hermes-agent
